def step_replace(text,number,new):
    pat=rf'^{re.escape(number)} .*?(?=\n\n|\Z)'
    text,n=re.subn(pat,lambda m:new,text,count=1,flags=re.M|re.S)
    if n!=1:raise RuntimeError('Missing step '+number)
    return text
raw=step_replace(raw,'2.1',r'''2.1 [Forward equation and initial data] Let $u_0\in W^{2,2}$ and $s\in\mathbb R$, put $w_0:=B_u(s)^{-1}u_0$, $w(t):=e^{-i(t-s)K/\hbar}w_0$ and $v(t):=B_u(t)w(t)$. By [F1] the group preserves $D(K)$, commutes with $K$ there and is strongly continuous both on $L^2$ and on $D(K)$ with graph norm: apply strong continuity to $w_0$ and $Kw_0$. That graph norm is equivalent to the $W^{2,2}$ norm, since the free operator has this equivalence and the bounded perturbation satisfies $\|K_0f\|\le\|Kf\|+\|V\|_\infty\|f\|$ and $\|Kf\|\le\|K_0f\|+\|V\|_\infty\|f\|$. Hence $w$ is continuous into $W^{2,2}$ and differentiable into $L^2$ with $w'=-iKw/\hbar$. The boosts are strongly continuous on $W^{2,2}$: translations are isometries in each weak-derivative norm and are continuous by smooth density, while derivatives of the affine-phase multiplier are constant multiples of that multiplier; its time dependence is a scalar phase. They are locally uniformly bounded on $W^{2,2}$. Thus $v$ is continuous into $W^{2,2}$. For differentiability, decompose its quotient into $B_u(t+h)(w(t+h)-w(t))/h+(B_u(t+h)-B_u(t))w(t)/h$; strong continuity on $L^2$, unitarity and step 1.2 give $v'=B_u(t)w'+i\lambda v-u\cdot\nabla v$, where $\lambda=m|u|^2/(2\hbar)$. By steps 1.3-1.4, $B_u(t)Kw=-\hbar^2\Delta v/(2m)+i\hbar u\cdot\nabla v+m|u|^2v/2+M_{V_u(\cdot,t)}v$. Therefore $i\hbar v'=-m|u|^2v/2-i\hbar u\cdot\nabla v+B_u(t)Kw=(-\hbar^2\Delta/(2m)+M_{V_u(\cdot,t)})v$; both extra terms cancel. At the actual initial time, $v(s)=B_u(s)w_0=u_0$, with no assumption that $s=0$. This proves existence and the initial condition in (b). [given, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, F1, F2, F4, F5, algebra]''')
raw=step_replace(raw,'2.2',r'''2.2 [Uniqueness with the transformed initial datum] Let $w$ be another solution in the stated class, and put $\rho(t):=B_u(t)^{-1}w(t)$. The continuity argument of step 2.1 applies also to the inverse boost. For its $L^2$ derivative, decompose the difference quotient just as in step 2.1, using the inverse derivative from step 1.2; this gives $\rho'=i\lambda\rho+u\cdot\nabla\rho+B_u(t)^{-1}w'$. The inverse transported identities of steps 1.3-1.4 give $B_u(t)^{-1}(-\hbar^2\Delta/(2m)+M_{V_u(\cdot,t)})B_u(t)\rho=K\rho-i\hbar u\cdot\nabla\rho+m|u|^2\rho/2$. Substituting the equation for $w$ cancels the scalar and first-order terms, so $i\hbar\rho'=K\rho$ and $\rho(s)=B_u(s)^{-1}u_0$. Set $G(t):=e^{i(t-s)K/\hbar}$ and $F(t):=G(t)\rho(t)$. The quotient decomposition $G(t+h)(\rho(t+h)-\rho(t))/h+(G(t+h)-G(t))\rho(t)/h$ converges in $L^2$: the first term by strong continuity and unitarity, the second by [F1] on the fixed vector $\rho(t)\in D(K)$. Hence $F'=G(t)(\rho'+iK\rho/\hbar)=0$. A differentiable Hilbert-space curve with zero derivative is constant, by applying the real scalar mean-value theorem to every bounded real linear functional. Thus $F(t)=F(s)=B_u(s)^{-1}u_0$, and $\rho(t)=e^{-i(t-s)K/\hbar}B_u(s)^{-1}u_0$. Multiplying by $B_u(t)$ proves $w(t)=B_u(t)e^{-i(t-s)K/\hbar}B_u(s)^{-1}u_0=v(t)$. This proves the uniqueness part of (b) with its actual initial datum. [given, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, step 2.1, F1, F5, algebra]''')
p.write_text(raw)
p=Path(f'items/{ids[3]}.md');raw=p.read_text();raw=raw.replace('B+ut_{j}','B-ut_{j}')
old=r'\frac{1}{2m}\sum_{k}P_{k}^{2} M_{V_{u}(\cdot,t)}';new=r'\frac{1}{2m}\sum_{k}P_{k}^{2}+M_{V_{u}(\cdot,t)}'
if raw.count(old)!=1:raise RuntimeError('Unexpected PDE')
raw=raw.replace(old,new)
raw=step_replace(raw,'2.1',r'''2.1 [Law transports] For a componentwise representative $f$, $(E_{Q_j}(B)B_u(t)f)(x)=\mathbf 1_B(x_j)e^{i\varphi(x,t)}f(x-ut)$, whereas $(B_u(t)E_{Q_j}(B-ut_j)f)(x)=e^{i\varphi(x,t)}\mathbf 1_{B-ut_j}(x_j-ut_j)f(x-ut)$. Since the two indicators agree, $E_{Q_j}(B)B_u(t)=B_u(t)E_{Q_j}(B-ut_j)$; taking norms gives $\mu^{Q_j}_{B_u(t)\psi}(B)=\mu^{Q_j}_{\psi}(B-ut_j)$. For momentum, changing variables $y=x-ut$ in the Fourier transform first on smooth compactly supported representatives yields $(U^{(s)}B_u(t)f)(p)=e^{-it u\cdot p/\hbar+i m|u|^2t/(2\hbar)}(U^{(s)}f)(p-mu)$. The equality extends to all $L^2$ by density and unitarity. Therefore the indicator $\mathbf 1_B(p_k)$ on the left equals the transported indicator $\mathbf 1_{B-mu_k}(p_k-mu_k)$ on the right, so $E_{P_k}(B)B_u(t)=B_u(t)E_{P_k}(B-mu_k)$ and $\mu^{P_k}_{B_u(t)\psi}(B)=\mu^{P_k}_{\psi}(B-mu_k)$. These are the two law transports in (b); neither depends on the phase in the position density. [step 1.2, step 1.3, F2, F3, algebra]''')
raw=step_replace(raw,'2.3',r'''2.3 [Moving-frame model and uniqueness] By [F5] the scalar bounded-potential operator $K$ has flow $e^{-itK/\hbar}$, and [F1] proves that $B_u(t)e^{-i(t-s)K/\hbar}B_u(s)^{-1}$ preserves $W^{2,2}$ and solves the additive equation $i\hbar v'=(\sum_kP_k^2/(2m)+M_{V(\cdot-ut)})v$ with $v(s)=v_0$. Assemble the finitely many components through the orthogonal-sum identification; the resulting $W_u(t,s)$ is unitary, preserves $W^{2,2}(\mathbb R^3;\mathbb C^r)$ and satisfies the displayed equation with $\psi(s)=\psi_0$. To verify uniqueness in the full stated class without an added continuity hypothesis in the $W^{2,2}$ norm, let $z$ be the difference of two $L^2$-differentiable, $W^{2,2}$-valued solutions with the same initial data. For each fixed $t$, the additive operator $A(t)=\sum_kP_k^2/(2m)+M_{V_u(\cdot,t)}$ is symmetric on $W^{2,2}$ because the free operator is self-adjoint and the real bounded multiplier is self-adjoint. Hence $d\|z(t)\|^2/dt=2\operatorname{Re}\langle-iA(t)z(t)/\hbar,z(t)\rangle=0$. The real mean-value theorem gives $\|z(t)\|^2=\|z(s)\|^2=0$ for every $t$. Thus the two curves agree, proving all of (d) for arbitrary initial time $s$. [step 1.1, F1, F5, algebra]''')
p.write_text(raw)
